00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.630 align:start position:0%
hundreds of indigenous protesters took  to the streets of atalaya do no  they demanded justice for indigenous  expert bruno pereira and british  journalist don phillips they went  missing a week ago while they were  returning from an expedition to the  javadi valley reservation  home to the largest number of  uncontacted tribes in the world  where are bruno and dom they chanted but  the demonstrators also denounced the  onslaught they have been suffering from  illegal fishermen poachers loggers and  miners  bruno pereira had been helping them  patrol their territory and map the  invasions dom phillips was accompanying  him on a trip to finish writing a book  on how to save the amazon  on sunday after a week-long search  divers retrieved a plastic bag from the  river containing a backpack and some  belongings of the two missing men  relatives say the backpack contained a  computer all belongings socks shirts  shorts their work tools such as their  laptops reports on monday morning that  two bodies had also been found or denied  by brazil's federal police  brazil's president jaibo sonaro said he  thought something evil had happened to  them  the evidence leads us to believe that  something bad was done to them because  human viscera were floating in the river  indigenous leaders we spoke to said they  will keep up the pressure to find out  what happened to dom phillips and bruno  pereira and that they will continue the  patrols that pedera organized to help  protect the rainforest  monica yanaikeval jazeera rio de janeiro
 

